  prose(<<~'CANONICAL_PROSE', origin: :published)
    ## Origins and Lineage
    
    The Wake-Singers are the surviving institutional form of orca shamanism, reduced by the Faction Wars from surface strongholds to a network of deep-water caves centered on the Corpse Current — the abyssal drift where, in their theology, the dead god's body still moves. They trace their shamanic lineage to Rrak-gruk-krul the Jaw-Breaker, whom they name as the first orca shaman. According to Wake-Singer tradition, Rrak-gruk-krul pried the Dreaming Tooth from the dead god's mandible while the divine blood was still cooling — an act that serves as foundational proof that the god possessed jaws, was flesh, was kin. The pod of Kzul-gnark-gnash rendered the cult's creation traditions into vibration-markings on whale-bone tablets, which constitute the oldest surviving record of the Dreaming Tooth's presence in the god's body.
    
    ## Theology and Practice
    
    The Wake-Singers believe that accumulated blood sacrifice will rouse the dead god from its slumber beneath the frozen sea. Their blood-rituals are framed not as worship but as medicine — the desperate ministrations of a pod attempting to resuscitate one of their own. In practice, this theology produces systematic violence: kidnapping parties targeting penguin fishing groups and settlements, underwater containment pens carved from living ice, and ritual drownings that leave surrounding waters discolored for days.
    
    Their shamans sing in frequencies that cause ice to fracture and penguin cardiac rhythms to destabilize — an effect well-documented in Aurora Stack defensive records. They wear headdresses carved from their own ancestors' bones, the carving performed while the bone retains warmth. Whether this last detail is literal or ceremonial exaggeration varies by source; I am inclined, given everything else I have read about Wake-Singer practice, to take it at face value.
    
    Grandmother Deep, the ancient entity residing in the Corpse Current, guides the cult's theology. The precise nature of this guidance — whether directive, oracular, or something the Wake-Singers impose on her silence — remains unclear from the available record.
    
    ## Leadership and Internal Divisions
    
    Operational authority rests with younger shamans, principally Vel'keth, who managed the logistical infrastructure of captive containment and ritual execution. The cult's senior political voice is Scar-Matriarch Keth'nal, described in multiple accounts as old enough to remember when orcas controlled the surface and penguins sheltered in snow-holes. Keth'nal's faction favors escalation — bolder raids on Aurora Stack settlements — while pragmatists within the cult argue they are overextended, having lost control of the Veil Vents at least once. The internal debate has not, by any account, caused defections. The theological stakes preclude departure.
    
    ## Major Operations
    
    | Event | Action | Outcome |
    |---|---|---|
    | **Shattered-Spire Assault** | Provided theological and operational coordination; hunt-songs focused through the Dreaming Tooth amplified pressure-magic against Aurora Stack's crystalline defenses | Assault on Aurora Stack; cult role was force-multiplier rather than primary combatant |
    | **Abyss Collapse Tide** | Three shamans killed when the ocean floor buckled during the cataclysm | Lost possession of the Dreaming Tooth; Grandmother Deep collected it and has not returned it |
    | **Tide Fall Blasphemy** | Ritual exploiting pressure-depth magic cracked memorial ice and raised water levels | Multiple Aurora Stack surface outposts drowned |
    | **Desecration of The Thewsed Slew~** | Tore open cleansed memorial ground with their own teeth | Three shamans died in the working; the aurora dimmed for three days (the Three-Day Dark) |
    | **Breach of Hearth Plaza / The Fissured Smelt~** | Accessed the plaza through deep tunnels opened by the Foedus∴vincu — the orca pact ratified by Light-Regent Xouhoulong with the Midnight Claws; descended through the corruption-scar | Vel'keth led forty chanters into unmapped passages connecting pact tunnels to the deepest corruption corridors |
    | **Three-Day Dark at The Fissured Smelt~** | Vel'keth performed a blood-ritual in the deep passages | Vel'keth was transformed into something accounts describe as no longer entirely mammalian; the party did not return to the surface |
    
    The Fissured Smelt~ breach warrants particular attention. Vel'keth's descent exploited routes that existed because of Xouhoulong's covert diplomacy with orca clans — a fact the Aurora Stack political establishment has been reluctant to examine in full. Whether the Wake-Singers found the god-corpse, found only ice, or remain singing in the deep is unresolved. On the ninth day following the breach, something ascended from the Glow-Fissure communicating in hunt-song frequencies structured as language. It was not Vel'keth. It addressed a Nightshelf reconnaissance unit by designation before the unit withdrew. The Nightshelf after-action report — which I have read, with difficulty, through their cipher conventions — confirms this detail without explaining it.
    
    ## Custodianship of the Omega Obelisk
    
    An unnamed traveler retrieved the Omega Obelisk — seven feet of whale-bone darkened by pressure-depths — from the Corpse Current, claiming it communicated through pressure-dreams. She delivered it to the Wake-Singers and drowned herself in scalding water three months later. The cult's custodianship devolved into containment: the Obelisk was housed in a deep chamber carved with old-script warnings, among them the surface-name of Augur ☽'Silvsiaelrous~ the Resolute, a Shadow Covens practitioner who had attempted to bury the artifact and was consumed by the Abyss Collapse Tide. When containment began failing — the Obelisk rising toward the chamber ceiling, surrounding ice weeping — a Communion mystic, ☽'Saetacaeia-Viamana~, descended and re-sealed it through a sacrifice of forbidden knowledge. The artifact eventually passed into the ☽'shadow Covens' Order's underground tunnels.
    
    ## Intelligence Operations
    
    The Gore Emissary, a Wake-Singer delegate, attended multi-faction trade negotiations at Junction Twelve beneath Midway Shelf. Rather than engage with trade proceedings, the Emissary used the chamber's acoustics to map branching tunnel networks. Wake-Singer chanters had been charting Nightshelf passages below the fourth depth for nine days prior — not raiding, mapping. After receiving ☽'ofund' Guild tunnel cartography through an arrangement brokered by Niaecum~, the chanters began singing penguin-built junction geometry into memory. This is, I think, the detail that should concern readers most. Blood-rituals are dramatic. Theology is contestable. But a faction that is memorizing the architecture of your tunnels is planning something that outlasts ritual.
    
    ## Current Status
    
    The Wake-Singers maintain operational presence at the Corpse Current. They hold alliances with Aureate and the Icebound Exchange — arrangements whose commercial logic I will not speculate on here — and remain hostile to the E'frostlings. They believe the god stirs. They cite pressure-shifts in the deep, anomalous behavior in captive penguins' blood, irregular aurora pulsing. They have sacrificed much and cannot afford for the deep to remain silent. Whether it will is not a question the archive answers.
  CANONICAL_PROSE
  annotation "note_1771575425865_0", anchor: "the desperate ministrations of a pod attempting to resuscitate one of their own", text: "The orca pressure-song term does not translate as 'resuscitate.' The closest rendering is 'warming' — the act of pressing bodies around a pod-member who has gone cold. The god is not dead in the way penguins mean dead. It is cooling. The Wake-Singers are not reviving a corpse; they are huddling around something they believe is still losing heat. The theological distinction is load-bearing: a body that is cooling can still be saved. A body that has cooled cannot. Everything they do follows from whether they are in time.", type: :pedantic, display: :full
  annotation "note_1771575425865_1", anchor: "Vel'keth led forty chanters into unmapped passages", text: "Forty. In a breach descent through corruption corridors. Counted by whom, exactly?", type: :skepticism, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771575425865_2", anchor: "It was not Vel'keth. It addressed a Nightshelf reconnaissance unit by designation", text: "I have grudgingly praised Nightshelf record-keeping before. Here it produced a document I have read four times and do not enjoy. The unit's tactical designation was assigned internally that same cycle — it had not been transmitted, posted, or spoken above the fourth depth. The entity used it correctly, including the cycle-suffix. The after-action report notes this without comment, in the flat procedural tone the Nightshelf use when they are extremely frightened. The unit leader filed for reassignment. It was denied.", type: :commentary, display: :full
  annotation "note_1771575425865_3", anchor: "alliances with Aureate and the Icebound Exchange — arrangements whose commercial logic I will not speculate on here", text: "The margins are precisely where I should have speculated. Aureate holds the Frost Zenith through exhaustion; the Exchange controls trade routes through institutional inertia. Both deal with a faction that drowns their species in ritual pens. Someone signs these agreements. Someone files them. Someone at Aureate looked at a ledger entry reading — what? Krill-route access? Deep-water passage rights? — and decided the arithmetic worked despite the source. How extraordinary that it always does. Commerce is the one theology on the Berg that requires no faith at all.", type: :commentary, display: :full
  annotation "note_1771575425865_4", anchor: "old enough to remember when orcas controlled the surface and penguins sheltered in snow-holes", text: "This predates the Clever Ice Age. By a considerable margin. Either Keth'nal's lifespan is unlike anything in the biological record, or 'remembers' is doing the same work here that it does in pressure-song tradition — carrying inherited memory as though it were personal experience. The distinction matters less to the Wake-Singers than it does to me.", type: :skepticism, display: :popout
